      "90": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "TRANSFORM\n\nNow it is time to get funky, we're adding a Transform node into the feedback loop.\nRemember that everything that happens in a feedback loop happens every frame.\nSo scaling up the image by 0.1 will eventually blow up the image to infinitely large proportions.\nThat is sort of the cool thing about feedback, everything moves into infinity.\n\nIn the example we've made a simple shape and a feedback loop.\nNext we added the transform the the feedback loop and exposed the inputs\n\nAssignment: play with the parameters and see how they affect the image. \nRemember: parameters can be controlled from the dashboard panel."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 1124.2130126953125,
          "width": 1930.18212890625,
          "x": 630.6151733398438,
          "y": 1427.2322998046875
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "ff02bbff",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Group",
        "thumbnail_visible": true
      },
      "91": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "EFFECTS\n\nYou are not limited to using a Transform in feedback loops.\nIn fact, any effect can be used in a feedback loop.\nYou could even feedback loop your feedback loop.\nBut do think to yourself: what would happen if I apply this effect infinitely ?\n\nIn this example we use Color Offset, which offsets each color channel with a given pixel count.\nNow the question rises: what happens if we infinitely offset a color? We get color rays! \nFor this reason we've also added a Vignette effect to \"contain\" the rays.\n\nWhen you get to the point where you use multiple effects and loops you will enter the field of experimentation and artistic expression. \nYou will quickly reach the point where you stop rationalizing which effects you should use and start experimenting until it looks right. \nThis is where the magic happens! Have fun!\n\nAssignment: open a new Wire project and create a patch using feedback and effects. Remember to have fun while you're at it!"
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 1209.8131103515625,
          "width": 1930.4542236328125,
          "x": 632.6744384765625,
          "y": 2603.162841796875
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "ff20c7bb",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Group",
        "thumbnail_visible": true
      },
      "92": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": true
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "FEEDBACK\n\nIn this tutorial we want to introduce you to the concept of video feedback.\nYou already have seen a couple of tutorials where this technique is used in the example sections.\nNow it is time to get your hands dirty and give feedback loops a try.\n\nThe concept is very simple.\nYou take a Video mixer and patch it back into itself.\nWire will create a delay node which is always necessary for a feedback loop.\nThe result is that every frame, the signal is sent back into itself allowing you to paint onto the screen or create trail-like effects.\n\nYou can get crazy creative with feedback loops by inserting effects into them, creating feedback loops within feedback loops or chain them together."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 302.46295166015625,
          "width": 795.7362670898438,
          "x": 632.1857299804688,
          "y": 274.96649169921875
        },
        "class": {
